.TH UZPACK 1 "2026-07-08" "v0.94" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
uzpack \- package binaries or scripts into self-extracting ELF32 executables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uzpack
.RI [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.RI [ \-v | \-\-version ]
.br
.B uzpack
.RI [ \-s | \-\-script ]
.RI [ \-u | \-\-update ]
.I origin
.RI [ destination [ .uzp ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uzpack
is a wrapper utility that converts a binary executable or a shell script
into a self-extracting, self-running ELF32 file using the
.BR uzpexec (1)
micro-stub loader.
The resulting file carries a 512-byte payload header (the
.B uzpexec
extractor) followed by a gzip-compressed copy of the original
.IR origin
file.  When executed, the stub decompresses the payload entirely in RAM
(via
.BR memfd_create (2))
and runs it without writing to disk.
.PP
The default output name is derived from the basename of
.IR origin
with the
.B .uzp
extension appended.  If
.I destination
is given, it is used as-is; the
.B .uzp
extension is conventional but not mandatory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display a brief usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version string embedded in the
.B uzpexec
stub and exit.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-script
Force script mode.  The internal stub is patched so that the payload is
interpreted by
.B /bin/sh
rather than executed as a raw binary.  If the input file begins with a
shebang
.RB ( #! ),
this mode is selected automatically.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
Update the embedded
.B uzpexec
payload inside the
.B uzpack
script itself.  This is primarily used during development when a new
.B uzpexec
binary is available.
.SH NOTES
.SS Origin and Form
The command
.B uzpack
is normally distributed as a compiled ELF32 binary produced from the
accompanying
.BR uzpack.sh
shell script.  The shell script is the canonical source form; the ELF32
binary is the derived, ready-to-run form.  Both forms behave identically
from the user's perspective.
.SS Inspecting Contents
Because the
.B .uzp
file consists of a 512-byte
.B uzpexec
stub followed immediately by gzip data, the compressed payload can be
inspected without execution:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dd skip=1 if=uzpack | zcat
.fi
.RE
.PP
This is a simple and quick way to verify the contents of a converted file.
.SS Script Compatibility
When packaging a shell script, the input should contain a properly set
shebang line (e.g.,
.BR #!/bin/sh ).
A universal template recommended for full compatibility is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#!/bin/sh
if [ \-t 0 ] || [ \-c /dev/tty ]; then exec </dev/tty; fi

# put your shell script code here

exit
.fi
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert a binary executable:
.PP
.RS
.nf
uzpack /usr/local/bin/myapp myapp.uzp
.fi
.RE
.PP
Convert a shell script (auto-detected by shebang):
.PP
.RS
.nf
uzpack deploy.sh
.fi
.RE
.PP
Convert the
.B uzpack
script itself into its own binary form:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sh uzpack.sh \-s uzpack.sh uzpack
./uzpack \-v
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uzpexec (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR zcat (1),
.BR memfd_create (2),
.BR dd (1)
